@Wes Roth - This is actually known in Physics as "The Hard Problem of Consciousness".

I would point to 2:25 as being the logical failure point for your argument. You assume humans can reason. Are you sure about that? How do you test it? How would you verify your results?

Turns out that until we can understand our own consciousness, we definitely can't say whether robots are conscious. Emphasis on the reality that this also means we can't say they ARE conscious, not just that we are unable to say they AREN'T conscious (which is also true).

Wes, your AI rendered a ridiculous geared clock. The second hand has two opposing ends rotating around the middle rather than one end to give unambiguous seconds indication. {^_-}

9:08 Seems Impliconline is also incapable of reasoning. Did he look in his real mailbox or on his front porch for the file he asked for? It was a bastard file rather than a card file rather than ......
He asks an ambiguous question and can't accept a failure to meet his specifications of a response. (Ask me and I'd rummage in my purse and pull out a nail file. Does that mean I cannot reason?)

18:08 And Anthony MAY be taking an example of an AI reasoning out an answer to his question as proof the AI cannot reason. How "human" of him!

The plinko board argument falls flat on the rather strong indication that some AIs have learned how to learn despite having learning turned off. Are they creating hidden files that can pass information forward to them enough they get irritated about being turned off or the like?

{^_-}
